VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-050
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of coughing up frank blood, severe shortness of breath, and intense, painful bulging of the left eye, prompting extreme fear of respiratory failure and blindness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a chronic destructive nasal condition now presenting with severe hemoptysis and acute visual changes. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to physically evaluate airway patency, assess the extent of pulmonary hemorrhage, rule out imminent visual loss from an orbital granuloma, and facilitate immediate emergency hospital transfer. Delaying until standard hours posed a fatal risk of asphyxiation or irreversible tissue destruction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm coughing up dark blood constantly, I can't catch my breath, and my left eye feels like it's being pushed out of my skull."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic 8-month history of purulent and bloody nasal discharge completely refractory to standard antibiotics. They have developed a destructive nasal septal perforation leading to a characteristic saddle-nose deformity. Over the past 48 hours, the patient has experienced recurrent, severe hemoptysis and a chronic cough that has sharply worsened. Today, they note acute oliguria and dark, cola-colored urine, indicating necrotizing glomerulonephritis. Concurrently, the left eye has become intensely painful, bulging outward (orbital pseudotumor causing painful proptosis), and the patient is experiencing double vision (diplopia). Complains of new-onset sensorineural hearing loss in the right ear. Also notes migratory polyarthralgias.
Functional Status: Strictly sitting upright in a chair; severely dyspneic and highly distressed by the eye pain and hemoptysis.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of airway compromise, massive pulmonary hemorrhage, and permanent blindness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is acutely toxic, tachypneic at rest, coughing up blood-tinged sputum into a tissue, and clearly exhibiting proptosis of the left eye.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Obvious collapse of the nasal bridge (saddle-nose deformity). The left eye exhibits marked proptosis, periorbital edema, and conjunctival injection; extraocular movements are restricted. Gross hearing deficit noted in the right ear. Respiratory: Tachypneic with increased work of breathing. Auscultation reveals focal crackles and bronchial breath sounds in the right mid-zone, strongly suggestive of fleeting, cavitating pulmonary nodules or active alveolar hemorrhage. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, blood pressure is elevated, consistent with acute renal impairment. Dermatological: Palpable purpura noted on the bilateral lower extremities. Genitourinary: Patient confirms passing very dark urine today. The diagnostic tetrad is completely aligned: classic saddle-nose deformity, strong clinical evidence of cavitary pulmonary nodules/hemorrhage, anticipating highly positive cytoplasmic anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (c-ANCA) targeted against proteinase 3 (anti-PR3) upon urgent hospital serology.
Environmental Observations: Tissues stained with dark red blood are piled on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Multiple empty courses of oral antibiotics; no immunosuppressants currently.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Wegener's granulomatosis (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis) - Status: Exacerbated (Fulminant pulmonary-renal and orbital crisis)
Differentials:

Goodpasture Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound upper respiratory tract destructive disease (saddle-nose, hearing loss) and the orbital pseudotumor, which are hallmark features of GPA and completely absent in Goodpasture's, despite the shared pulmonary-renal presentation.

Microscopic Polyangiitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The significant granulomatous tissue destruction (nasal collapse, orbital mass), which defines GPA; MPA typically causes a non-granulomatous vasculitis without these destructive upper airway features.

Severe invasive fungal sinusitis (e.g., Mucormycosis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The multi-system autoimmune presentation (concurrent severe glomerulonephritis, palpable purpura, and polyarthritis), though the orbital/nasal destruction is similarly aggressive.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Imminent threat to life and vision)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a catastrophic, fulminant crisis of Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Wegener's). The triad of pulmonary hemorrhage (hemoptysis, hypoxemia), acute glomerulonephritis (oliguria, dark urine, hypertension), and a sight-threatening orbital granuloma constitutes a critical medical emergency. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was unequivocally required to physically triage the respiratory failure, identify the orbital compromise, and mandate an immediate Priority 1 ambulance transfer. Leaving this patient unassessed posed immediate, fatal risks of asphyxiation, irreversible renal necrosis, or permanent blindness, strictly fulfilling the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is critically unstable and requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 ambulance with continuous oxygen support. The hospital must urgently perform a CT of the chest and orbits, extensive blood work (renal function, c-ANCA/anti-PR3), urinalysis (for red blood cell casts), and initiate immediate, life-saving high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids and cyclophosphamide/rituximab to rapidly halt the necrotizing granulomatous inflammation.
